meta {
  name: List Platform Carriers
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/platform/carriers
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{platformAccessToken}}
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 200) {
    const allstate = res.getBody().carriers.find((c) => c.name === "Allstate");
    if (allstate) bru.setVar("allstateCarrierId", allstate.id);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("carries the seeded catalog", function () {
    // Seeded by the core seed (`carriers.seed.ts`), not by any migration, so
    // this holds on every environment.
    const names = res.getBody().carriers.map((c) => c.name);
    expect(names).to.include("Allstate");
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /platform/carriers — the global carrier catalog

  Backs the Super Admin onboarding wizard's carrier picker, and is the read half
  of the eventual super-admin carrier CRUD (PAC-93).

  - **Module:** none (`@SkipModule()`)
  - **Permission:** `platform:agencies:read`
  - **Data scope:** none — above the tenant boundary. `@SkipTenant()` and
    `@SkipBranch()` are both set.

  ## Why this exists next to `GET /carriers`

  `/carriers` is gated on the `deal_audits` / `crm_service` **modules**, and a
  platform operator has no agency, so no entitlement can satisfy it — widening
  its permission list would not help. This route is what `carrier.schema.ts`
  reserved for super-admin access.

  It returns **globals only** (`agencyId: null`), which is exactly the set a
  carrier appointment may target: appointments are unique across tenants, so an
  agency-scoped custom carrier would make "the same carrier" undecidable.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  { "carriers": [ { "id": "6a86…", "name": "Allstate",
                    "policyNumberPattern": "\\d+", "policyNumberHint": null } ] }
  ```

  ## Errors
  - `401 Unauthorized` — missing/expired token.
  - `403 Forbidden` — not a platform account.
}
